                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[i]* A free key of the game was given to me. Even so, that didn’t affect my opinion of the final product.[/i]\n\nThis game was a nice surprise. [i]Dungeon Girl[/i] is an adventure puzzle RPG with kinetic visual novel features developed by the Japanese group [url=https://twitter.com/Inu_to_Neko]Inu to Neko[/url]. You play as a cute girl called Monica, and your objective is to conquer all the floors of a dungeon, up to 1.000. It's very simple: you just match symbols like \"attack\", \"heal\" and others to perform actions. You can also get help from your party: you can unlock a total of ten characters and have three of them set as support members. \n\nThe more you explore through the dungeon together with them, the more \"friendship points\" you'll get and you'll be able to unlock new skills of your friends. You can also unlock short stories featuring those characters; they don't have an impact on the main story, they're basically just for fun. Besides those points, you can also get money, items, etc. There's also an option to mix items in order to make them stronger or to create entirely new things to help you through your battles. According to the developers, there are around 300 items to craft and 200 skills to use. I think that a guide would be very useful, and I wonder if it's ever going to be made (by the developers themselves or a regular player). [b]I think it's worth mentioning that Dungeon Girl can get kind of hard after a while.[/b] But that's probably me, I like games like this one but I really suck at them too, lol. I also recommend saving your game with a certain frequency, you never know when a powerful monster will show up and drag your face across the floor. \n\nThe art style is pleasing and consistent. But, at least in my opinion, the animation during the story mode seemed a bit \"stiff\". The characters basically have one sprite pose, the only variation is their facial expressions. Still, sometimes their expressions will go unchanged regardless of the situation, and that kind of bothered me a little. Maybe some people won't mind that, I guess that's more like a pet peeve of mine. BGM was alright, but it gets very repetitive after a while. Also, this game has partial voice acting: Monica is the only voiced character, but she only speaks during her battles inside the dungeon. She will mostly say a few words instead of a whole phrase. It would have been nice if all characters were voiced too.\n\nOverall, this game is cute, simple, and it's visually appealing. There are a few problems, though: Dungeon Girl has an intuitive menu and gameplay, and there's a brief explanation of how the game works. However, I think there could have been a step by step tutorial to help players since you just have to guess how things work when you're creating a new item or even when you're using a skill in battle. The resolution of the game is also quite low, and there's no resize option. I haven't finished this game yet, and so far it took me six hours to reach floor 75 and unlock eight of the ten support members. \n\n7/10. If you like puzzles and match-3 games, you'll enjoy Dungeon Girl! It's all about strategy and some patience. This game can also be very addicting, it's one of those titles where you say \"I'll only play it for a few minutes\" and you end up playing for hours straight. Also, don't expect a deep story and more depth to the characters' personality, this title is heavily focused on the gameplay and the story is kind of \"just there\" in the end.",
